<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leonid Brezhnev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1906">1906</a>–<a href="/years/1982">1982</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leonid Brezhnev led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> as first secretary, later general secretary, of the Communist Party from 1964 until his death in 1982. His long rule brought stability for officials, a military build-up to nuclear parity with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and détente in the 1970s, but also economic stagnation and corruption. He crushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-spring">Prague Spring</a> in 1968, justified by the Brezhnev Doctrine, and sent troops into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> in 1979.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leonid-brezhnev.jpg" alt="Leonid Brezhnev" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vladimir Musaelyan · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leonid_Brezhnev,_Staatshoofden,_portretten,_Bestanddeelnr_925-6564_(cropped)(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
